<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serres</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Serres is a city in the region of Macedonia in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, in the fertile valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Strymon</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> took it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in 1345, and after his death it became the seat of his widow Helena and of the despot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jovan-uglesa">Jovan Uglješa</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> captured it in 1383, and it was part of their empire until 1913.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/serres.jpg" alt="Serres" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Мико · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saints_Theodore_Tyro_and_Theodore_Stratelates_Church,_Serres_072.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Serres stands on a fertile plain at an elevation of about 70 metres, some 24 kilometres north-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Strymon</a> river and 69 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>. The ancient city was built on a high, steep hill known as Koulas, just north of the modern one, and controlled the land road that followed the Strymon valley from the Strymonian Gulf to the Danubian countries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> mentions it as Siris in the 5th century BCE; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> later calls it Sirae, and the name has stayed plural since.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known inhabitants were the Bryges and Strymonians, followed by the Paeonian tribes of the Siropaiones and Odomantes. In the Roman period the city, called Sirra, was an important city of the province of Macedonia and the seat of a federation of five cities, the Pentapolis. It flourished under the Pax Romana, declined in the crisis of 235 to 284 CE and recovered under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>. Its first attested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> took part in the Second Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> in 449.</p><p>In about 803 Emperor Nikephoros I rebuilt the town and installed a garrison against the Slavic tribes. It was pillaged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> in the 10th century and taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-i">Ivan Asen I</a>'s forces after the Byzantine defeat at the Battle of Serres in the late 12th century. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan of Bulgaria</a>, who according to George Akropolites reduced it to a small settlement around the citadel.</p><p>The Nicaean Empire took it in 1246, and by the 14th century Nikephoros Gregoras called it large and marvellous. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> took it on 25 September 1345, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a>'s death in 1355 it became the Principality of Serres, ruled first by the Empress-dowager Helena and from 1365 by the Despot Jovan Uglješa, whose administration worked in Greek.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> took Serres definitively on 19 September 1383, and it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Evrenos Beg. In the 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a> recorded a prosperous town with 2,000 shops in its market. A fire in 1849 destroyed most of its churches. A Bulgarian army took the city on 6 November 1912, and Greek forces entered it on 11 July 1913; the retreating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> set fire to it. In 1943 its Jewish population was deported to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treblinka-extermination-camp">Treblinka</a> death camp.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Serres is the capital of the Serres regional unit and the second largest city of Central Macedonia after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>. Its municipal population was 70,703 in 2021. It is a trade centre for tobacco, grain and livestock, and it hosts a campus of the International Hellenic University and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the Aristotle University of Thessaloniki. The Archaeological Museum is housed in an Ottoman bezesten.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Serres” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That year at <b>Serres</b> he proclaimed himself Tsar, and on 16 April 1346 in Skopje he was crowned Emperor of the Serbs and Greeks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Stefan Dušan's death in 1355, his widow, Empress Jelena, ruled <b>Serres</b>, the city he had taken from Byzantium in 1345.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-serres">Despotate of Serres</a></li></ul></section>
